# an ending
@title The Crown's Shadow
@bg field
@mood dread

narrate He did the counting — properly, the way he had been taught by the best practitioner of it who ever lived — and the counting said: one man, all of it, and the rest of them get to keep their best things.
narrate So he did it alone.
narrate And there was nothing left of the boy who had got up in Sallow's Rest.
narrate What stood in the field afterwards was a man with a clean hand and no memory and an enormous amount of power and no idea why any of it mattered.
narrate He went back to Ashford, and took the title, and the commission, and the house.
narrate He held the Fever as a threat. Not because he was wicked. Because he had forgotten why he had ever wanted it ended.
narrate In the eleventh year there was a girl in the Mire who asked a question in a village square.
narrate Corin had her brought to Ashford, and sat her down, and made her tea.
corin gentle "I understand."
narrate And she did not come back.

@ending crown
